<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Oykel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Oykel is a river of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-highlands">Scottish Highlands</a>, rising near Ben More <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assynt">Assynt</a> and flowing east to the Kyle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutherland">Sutherland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dornoch-firth">Dornoch Firth</a>. For much of its course it forms the boundary between the historic counties of Sutherland and Ross-shire, and it is noted for its salmon fishing. Ptolemy's Smertae probably lived in the valleys of its upper course.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-oykel.jpg" alt="River Oykel" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Le Deluge · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strathoykel_at_Tuiteam_Tarbhach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The river, Òiceall in Scottish Gaelic, rises at about 460 metres on the southern side of Ben More Assynt, a few miles from Ullapool, and flows for just over 56 kilometres. Its upper valley, Glen Oykel, is mostly desolate moorland, and its lower valley is Strath Oykel. The River Cassley joins it at Invercassley to form the Kyle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutherland">Sutherland</a>, which is later joined by the River Shin and the River Carron before it discharges into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dornoch-firth">Dornoch Firth</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name is of uncertain derivation and may be Pictish, perhaps from an element meaning lively, as in the River Ogwen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, or from a word for ridge; a link with the Welsh uchel has been judged unlikely. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> knew the river as the Ekkjal. It divided the ancient Pictish province of Cat, covering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutherland">Sutherland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>, from the province of Ross, and kept that role until both were merged into the Highland Region in 1975.</p><p>Traditionally it also marked the line between the parish of Kincardine to the south and the parish of Creich to the north. In 1406 the Mackays defeated the Clan MacLeod of Lewis at the Battle of Tuiteam Tarbhach on its north bank, near the mouth of the Tutim Burn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The A837 road runs along the river for most of its length and crosses it at Oykel Bridge, about 10 kilometres west of Rosehall, where the hotel of the same name lodges the anglers who fish the river. It is designated a Special Area of Conservation and has abundant stocks of Atlantic salmon, of which anglers caught over 1,000 in 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “River Oykel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Smertae are known solely from Ptolemy's Geography of about 150, which places them inland in the far north of Scotland, usually located in central Sutherland around the upper <b>Oykel</b> and Shin valleys.</blockquote><a href="/state/smertae">Smertae</a></li></ul></section>
